{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Joseph remembered the dreams which he had dreamed about them</b> — about them, and he knew that they had been fulfilled, for behold they bowed down to him; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 42:9). In my opinion the matter is the reverse, for Scripture says that when Joseph saw his brothers bowing down to him, he remembered all the dreams that he had dreamed about them, and he knew that not one of them had been fulfilled on this occasion, for he knew from their interpretation that all his brothers would bow down to him first from the first dream — \"and behold, we were binding sheaves\" — for \"we\" alludes to all his eleven brothers, and a second time the sun and the moon and eleven stars would bow down to him, from the second dream. And since he did not see Benjamin with them, he devised this stratagem to bring a false accusation against them in order that they would also bring Benjamin his brother to him, to fulfill the first dream first. And therefore he did not want to say to them, \"I am Joseph your brother,\" and to say, \"Hurry and go up to my father,\" and that he should send wagons, as he did with them the second time — for his father would come immediately without doubt. And after the first dream was fulfilled, he revealed himself to them, to fulfill the second dream. And had it not been so, Joseph would have committed a great sin by causing pain to his father and leaving him many days in bereavement and mourning over Simeon and over him. And even if his intention was to cause his brothers some distress, how could he not have compassion for his father's old age? But he did everything well, in its proper time, to fulfill the dreams, for he knew that they would truly be fulfilled. Also the second matter that he did to them with the goblet was not that his intention was to cause them pain, but he suspected that perhaps they harbored hatred toward Benjamin — that they would be jealous of him on account of their father's love, as they had been jealous of him — or perhaps Benjamin sensed that they had a hand in Joseph's fate, and a quarrel and hatred was born among them, and therefore he did not want Benjamin to go with them, lest they lay a hand on him; so he tested them regarding their love for him. And to this our Rabbis intended in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 93:9): Rabbi Chiya bar Abba said, all the words that you read that Judah spoke before his brothers, until you reach \"and Joseph could not refrain himself,\" contained appeasement for Joseph, appeasement for his brothers, appeasement for Benjamin — appeasement for Joseph: see how he offers his life for the children of Rachel, etc. And so I say that all these matters were in Joseph, from his wisdom in the interpretation of the dreams. For one may wonder: after Joseph had remained in Egypt many days and had been an overseer and a ruler in the house of a great lord in Egypt, how did he not send a single letter to his father to inform him and comfort him? For Egypt is close to Hebron, about six days' journey, and even if it were a year's journey it would have been proper to inform him out of honor for his father, and how precious is the ransom of his life — and he would ransom him with abundant wealth. But he saw that the bowing down of his brothers to him, and also his father and all his offspring with him, could not come about in their land, and he hoped that he would be there in Egypt, when he saw his great success there — and all the more so after he heard Pharaoh's dream, for it became clear to him that they would all come there and all his dreams would be fulfilled." "<b>And he said to them: You are spies</b> — This accusation must contain a reason or a pretext, for what did they do that he should say thus to them, when the whole land came to him and they were among those who came, as it says, \"to buy grain among those who came, for the famine was in the land of Canaan.\" And perhaps he saw them as men of fine appearance and distinguished, all dressed in elegant garments, and he said to them: it is not the way of distinguished men such as you to come to buy food, for you have many servants. And it is possible that they were among the first to come from the land of Canaan, and this is the meaning of \"And the sons of Israel came to buy grain among those who came, for the famine was in the land of Canaan\" — for now the first ones came from there, \"and Joseph said to them: You are spies,\" for from the land of Canaan no one had come to me to buy food. And this is the meaning of \"From where have you come?\" that he said to them at the outset."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:15.589187131Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}